WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.983
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.983 --> 00:00:13.592
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Hey.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.350
&gt;&gt; Hi, Jeffrey.
&gt;&gt; Hi, how are you?

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:20.062
&gt;&gt; We're doing good, everybody's excited.

00:00:20.062 --> 00:00:21.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you so

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.260
much for coming all the way up from LA.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:25.410
We appreciate the visit.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:26.600
&gt;&gt; Excited to be here.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.110
&gt;&gt; We're very excited to have you.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.170
That was an incredible
list of accomplishments,

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.790
and you started off
without getting a degree.

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:37.230
Can you tell us why?

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:38.998
&gt;&gt; I will, but I actually.

00:00:38.998 --> 00:00:44.340
I've always found in speaking
in front of any groups,

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.219
it's actually valuable for me to know
a little bit about the audience.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:55.385
And so, if I could just do a really
quick survey of how many forms are there

00:00:55.385 --> 00:01:00.594
here that are headed to Vegas tonight,
could I see a show of hands?

00:01:00.594 --> 00:01:01.562
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Boy.

00:01:01.562 --> 00:01:06.411
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I would just want to give you sort

00:01:06.411 --> 00:01:11.087
of two things that I might be able
to help you out on the first is

00:01:11.087 --> 00:01:15.858
likely probably not a lot of people
in this room that could give

00:01:15.858 --> 00:01:20.309
you wardrobe advice as to how to dress for
the 1970s.

00:01:20.309 --> 00:01:23.358
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I actually can do that,

00:01:23.358 --> 00:01:26.550
I have knowledge and experience there.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:31.010
And probably more importantly than that.

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:36.330
You should know that right around

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:41.580
the corner from the marquee club at

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:47.170
the cosmopolitan,
is the best pizza in Las Vegas.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.280
It's called Secret Pizza.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:55.240
It is a secret, but now there are couple
of hundred of view that know the secret.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:59.982
It's open till 5:00 AM, which is
I'm sure right around the time and

00:01:59.982 --> 00:02:03.414
which you'll be wanting and on some pizza.

00:02:03.414 --> 00:02:06.212
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I did not go to college.

00:02:06.212 --> 00:02:08.002
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:08.002 --> 00:02:13.370
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:19.520
&gt;&gt; The interesting thing that I

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.660
partnered in 1994 with Steven Spielberg
and David Gavin, the three of us do,

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.450
actually do not have a college degree,
so whatever that's worth for [LAUGH].

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.070
I didn't go to college,
because I actually started working.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:36.950
Very young as a teenager
I had volunteered for

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:42.410
a man who ran for
mayor of New York City, John Lindsay,

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.790
who had been a Congressman
on the Upper East Side.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:52.550
And Lindsay was really
an incredibly charismatic,

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:59.380
very forward thinking, very, I think

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:06.600
engaging and thoughtful,
unique liberal politician.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.810
And came in a time and

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:14.910
an era where participating, and
giving, and being involved in.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:20.940
Civic, you know was pretty powerful,
you know, the late sixties were,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.990
you know,
a time of a lot of civil rights movements

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.060
changing moments in our culture and
our society, and being there

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.370
in politics,
in government was a very exciting thing.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:42.430
And I started very young as a teenager,
and continued to work, and

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.310
when that moment,
I actually did go to college for a moment.

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:52.020
I registered at NYU, because it was
the closest college to City Hall.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:58.726
And in that era you only had
to hand your class card in,

00:03:58.726 --> 00:04:06.044
and then show up for exams, so it was
pretty easy for me to go to college.

00:04:06.044 --> 00:04:09.883
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think the first, in 1970,

00:04:09.883 --> 00:04:14.557
the first mid term exam
overlapped with the first

00:04:14.557 --> 00:04:18.695
police strike in a modern city in America.

00:04:18.695 --> 00:04:21.820
[LAUGH] The police went on
strike in New York City,

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:23.570
which had never happened before.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:28.620
And I thought, I could do better
there than in a college classroom,

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.108
and that was the end of my college career.

00:04:30.108 --> 00:04:33.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] What did you

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:36.330
learn from those years that you've
taken interior career later?

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:40.818
&gt;&gt; Everything, I mean, it was just,
it was a lessons in life.

00:04:40.818 --> 00:04:44.007
And about people, and values, and

00:04:44.007 --> 00:04:48.685
seen the harsh realities
of the streets of New York,

00:04:48.685 --> 00:04:52.530
and the goods, and bads, and the uglies.

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:56.981
And honestly there's not in those five,
or six, seven years that I worked for

00:04:56.981 --> 00:05:00.090
the mayor, I actually saw everything.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:04.410
I was a advance man for

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:08.590
the mayor and who loved to be
on the streets of New York,

00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:11.610
felt that he should be there whenever,

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:16.620
wherever anything exceptional,
or extraordinary was happening,

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:21.190
and it required the resources of
government to be there and respond.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:26.290
So, every time and anytime anything
bad happened, he wanted to show up and

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:28.630
make sure the government
was there doing its job.

00:05:28.630 --> 00:05:32.810
So, whether it's a tenement would
burn down, or subway accident, or

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:37.380
a police officer shot in the line of duty,
or you know what a main breaker.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:42.230
Anything that was at all disruptive,
he felt as the leader of the city,

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:46.600
he should be there front and
center to see that the city did it's job.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.280
And so, for me as an advance man,

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:54.130
it pretty much put me in the wrong place
at the wrong time, pretty much everyday.

00:05:54.130 --> 00:06:00.270
So, you'll learn about amazing,
amazing things about life and death, and

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:07.140
moments of happiness, and
sacrifice and just everything.

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:10.130
It's just the entire rainbow of colors

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:14.120
of everything around life
reflected in those experiences.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:19.010
And I think, they that's served me very
well and they matured me in a way,

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:23.180
and they gave me a confidence
about of myself.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:28.120
I think which was unique and
for somebody so young.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:32.943
I had these life experiences
at an extremely early age.

00:06:32.943 --> 00:06:36.209
&gt;&gt; And a sense of responsibility as
a leader that would [CROSSTALK].

00:06:36.209 --> 00:06:37.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you could make a difference.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:42.060
This idea that you know,
you can't change the course of things.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.630
I don't buy that of anything, but

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:50.140
particularly, I think that, and
even today I think about it,

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:53.340
people feel like, well, if you don't
like something, what do you do about it?

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.960
And I would say, well,
you have two things.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.070
And they are invaluable, and

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:03.380
I think, we tend not to understand how
important and how valuable they are.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.340
It's your voice and your vote.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:10.380
And so, right now, today,
we're sort of going sideways here.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:15.220
For me in politics today, I don't think
there's ever been a more important time in

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:17.950
which we have to exercise our voice and
our vote.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:20.270
And we have elections that
are coming up later this year.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:25.140
We have elections that are coming up in
a little more than two and a half years.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:30.090
And for me in terms of what
I'm more focus on it than

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:35.090
anything else today is I
would love to see this next

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:39.900
election cycle, these next two
election cycles particularly 2020.

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:45.120
I would like to see a participation and
a turnout in our democracy,

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:47.730
the lives of which our country
has never experienced before,

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:50.380
because the stakes have never been higher.

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:53.600
And so, through this one in particular,

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:58.630
there's nothing more important than voter
registration and voter participation.

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:00.210
It is the foundation.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.888
Of our democracy, and it is a choice.

00:08:02.888 --> 00:08:07.789
It is a moment in time in which we
actually get to determine the course of

00:08:07.789 --> 00:08:09.876
our country and our leaders.

00:08:09.876 --> 00:08:14.723
And we only have ourselves to thank or
blame for where we are.

00:08:16.945 --> 00:08:19.553
Go vote.

00:08:19.553 --> 00:08:25.469
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:25.469 --> 00:08:29.680
&gt;&gt; So that call to foster expression and

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:32.360
to hear voices,
is that what brought you into Hollywood?

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:36.560
&gt;&gt; No, I wanted [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:39.759
&gt;&gt; No, I was 22-years-old.

00:08:39.759 --> 00:08:41.870
And so Lindsay ran for president in 1972.

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:48.556
I worked in Wisconsin and Florida,
which were the two primaries.

00:08:48.556 --> 00:08:53.231
When he lost in those primaries and
went back to city government,

00:08:53.231 --> 00:08:56.300
I did not want to go
back to city government.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:59.979
because I felt like I had done
everything that I could do, and

00:08:59.979 --> 00:09:01.710
I wanted a new challenge.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:06.088
At 22-years-old, I really had sort of

00:09:06.088 --> 00:09:10.716
risen as far as was
practical in government.

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:15.415
I don't think I was going to get made
police commissioner or fire commissioner.

00:09:15.415 --> 00:09:18.506
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so I wanted to go find a career

00:09:18.506 --> 00:09:22.480
in an industry where at least
age would not pull me back.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:26.634
And many people said go
look at entertainment.

00:09:26.634 --> 00:09:31.959
Look at the movie business
because it is a place

00:09:31.959 --> 00:09:38.690
in which youth is actually
a valued asset not a liability.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:42.701
And so that that is actually
sort of the the big idea of why.

00:09:42.701 --> 00:09:46.350
And I got a couple little opportunities.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:51.669
But then I had the really great
fortune of meeting this amazing,

00:09:51.669 --> 00:09:56.399
amazing person, Barry Diller,
who invented something

00:09:56.399 --> 00:10:01.641
called Movies of the Week,
which we don't have anymore in TV.

00:10:01.641 --> 00:10:04.470
But he was real both creative and
an entrepreneur.

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:09.370
And he had just been made the chairman
Paramount Pictures at the age

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.900
of 34-years-old,
which was rather extraordinary.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:16.645
And Barry hired me as his gofer,

00:10:16.645 --> 00:10:22.490
which I think when I went to work for
him I was 23.

00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:29.760
And then over the course of the next
11 years that I worked at Paramount and

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:34.210
I worked for Barry,
he did one of the most amazing things,

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:37.800
which is he really genuinely mentored me.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.030
He invested in me.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:40.420
He believed in me.

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:46.530
And he in a very, really sort of
almost thoughtful, methodical way.

00:10:46.530 --> 00:10:51.840
I didn't understand it at the time, but he
had me work in every area of the company.

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.060
And so by the time I was 29-years-old,
and I'd worked for six years.

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:58.935
I was in marketing, and
distribution, and international, and

00:10:58.935 --> 00:11:00.198
negative pickups, and TV.

00:11:00.198 --> 00:11:04.031
I had all these experiences where I would
work for a year or so in one of these, and

00:11:04.031 --> 00:11:06.015
then he'd move me into something else.

00:11:06.015 --> 00:11:09.070
And I didn't see any
grand plan here in this.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:12.990
I was just getting moved
around a bit [LAUGH].

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:17.940
But when I was 29-years-old, 30-years-old,
he made me president of the studio.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:22.060
And I actually could do the job.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:27.061
And the only reason I could do the job
is because he had taken the time

00:11:27.061 --> 00:11:32.262
to actually train me and give me
the resources to function in the job.

00:11:32.262 --> 00:11:37.170
&gt;&gt; Can you tell us a little bit about
a day in the life as the head of a studio?

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:41.401
&gt;&gt; Well, yeah,
I'll focus just maybe narrowly on

00:11:41.401 --> 00:11:46.157
movies because that was what
I was doing at Paramount.

00:11:46.157 --> 00:11:51.589
When I went to Disney, it was sort
of a broader job because it involved

00:11:51.589 --> 00:11:56.850
television, and animation, and
much wider responsibilities.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:02.610
But at Paramount,
running a studio really is about

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.490
finding great ideas and
finding great talent.

00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:12.748
Creating a great environment for
people to be able to pursue and

00:12:12.748 --> 00:12:18.644
realize dreams,
even though those dreams, in many cases,

00:12:18.644 --> 00:12:23.632
are anywhere between improbable and
impossible.

00:12:23.632 --> 00:12:28.815
And to create an environment where

00:12:28.815 --> 00:12:36.330
people have the ability to fail and
to take risk.

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:41.268
And the reason for
that is that great stories,

00:12:41.268 --> 00:12:48.368
great movie telling, is at its
best when its original and unique.

00:12:48.368 --> 00:12:54.949
And if you sort of think about it
almost as a mathematical equation,

00:12:54.949 --> 00:12:59.232
original and unique equals risky, right.

00:12:59.232 --> 00:13:04.621
It's not a proven formula.

00:13:04.621 --> 00:13:08.660
And risky equals,
at least to some degree, failure.

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:13.352
So if you don't allow some failure in
the equation, you're not going to have

00:13:13.352 --> 00:13:18.059
people do things that are risky and
unique, that are unique and original.

00:13:18.059 --> 00:13:22.081
And if you don't have unique and original,
everything becomes derivative and

00:13:22.081 --> 00:13:24.670
I think uninspired, and
you won't have success.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:27.650
So creating a great environment for

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:33.400
people to take that appropriate amount
of risk and to actually be able

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:37.655
to have the ambition to do things
that sometimes aren't going to work.

00:13:37.655 --> 00:13:42.325
Now, very specifically,

00:13:42.325 --> 00:13:45.340
I don't, maybe it came from those years.

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:50.990
I have an insane work ethic and habit,

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:56.074
and I get up very early in the morning.

00:13:56.074 --> 00:14:00.838
As some people may have heard, I have
multiple breakfasts, multiple lunches,

00:14:00.838 --> 00:14:02.038
multiple dinners.

00:14:02.038 --> 00:14:03.915
And how about that, right.

00:14:03.915 --> 00:14:06.254
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So that's genetic,

00:14:06.254 --> 00:14:08.600
I can thank my parents for that.

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:11.480
I sleep five hours a night,
I thank my parents for that.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:13.510
I don't get jet lag,
I thank my parents for that.

00:14:13.510 --> 00:14:14.880
This is all in the gene pool.

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:16.790
I have nothing to do with it.

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:18.550
If you only sleep five hours a night,

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:21.112
which I have done since
I was 15-years-old.

00:14:21.112 --> 00:14:22.920
The Beatles actually wrote a song for me.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:25.350
It's called Eight Days a Week,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:26.445
&gt;&gt; That was for me, anyway.

00:14:26.445 --> 00:14:29.930
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I got an extra day, and

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:33.090
I manage my time, I invest my time.

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:36.354
Nobody's ever actually asked me, so
why do you have all these breakfasts,

00:14:36.354 --> 00:14:37.410
lunches and dinners?

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:38.190
I'm going to tell you,

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:42.150
including some of my partners who
are sitting here in front of me.

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:46.304
I have found, as a head of a studio,

00:14:46.304 --> 00:14:54.211
there's a very great distance
between you and talent and artists.

00:14:54.211 --> 00:14:57.701
And, again, there's a little bit of fear.

00:14:57.701 --> 00:15:00.795
And as you grow,
both in the job that you have, but

00:15:00.795 --> 00:15:05.300
also reputationally, and
they create mythology around you.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:08.080
And coming to see me over the years,

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:11.000
it started to get to the point
where it's like going to see Oz.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:12.842
It scares you to death.

00:15:12.842 --> 00:15:17.368
And I found that the way
to take that out of

00:15:17.368 --> 00:15:22.308
the equation was across
a table with a meal.

00:15:22.308 --> 00:15:26.832
And that suddenly,
just the relaxed nature of sitting at

00:15:26.832 --> 00:15:31.733
a table with somebody coming and
bringing food, and eating,

00:15:31.733 --> 00:15:37.105
just gave me an ability to connect
with people way, way, way better

00:15:37.105 --> 00:15:43.160
than I could across a desk in an office or
sitting on the other side of a couch.

00:15:43.160 --> 00:15:45.961
And so
I just found that I'm way more productive.

00:15:45.961 --> 00:15:49.054
And so I would get up and
I would have breakfast at six, seven,

00:15:49.054 --> 00:15:50.194
eight in the morning.

00:15:50.194 --> 00:15:56.330
All three of those by the way, [LAUGH].

00:15:56.330 --> 00:16:01.070
So in the studio,
a typical Monday morning,

00:16:01.070 --> 00:16:05.750
we would, the first thing we would do,
is have a weekend read.

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:08.120
So probably,

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:13.580
maybe the most important valuable thing as
an executive that you have to do is read.

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:20.430
And I think that all great
movies start with a great idea.

00:16:20.430 --> 00:16:23.620
All great ideas must have a great story.

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:25.800
And so reading is essential.

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:32.224
And I will tell you just how demanding
it was as a typical week for

00:16:32.224 --> 00:16:36.653
me when I was at Paramount is
I would actually read ten or

00:16:36.653 --> 00:16:41.492
12 screenplays a week, and
I'm not a superfast reader.

00:16:41.492 --> 00:16:46.328
I'm dyslexic, and so
I actually have to read a little slow.

00:16:46.328 --> 00:16:53.370
And so it would take me almost
two hours to read a screenplay.

00:16:53.370 --> 00:16:58.049
And I used to actually come start at
6 o'clock on Saturday morning and

00:16:58.049 --> 00:17:01.141
read till 5,
6 o'clock in the afternoon and

00:17:01.141 --> 00:17:05.609
then comeback probably do about
half of that on a Sunday for years.

00:17:05.609 --> 00:17:09.690
I mean years and years, and years I have
read thousands of screenplays literally.

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:15.275
And so then on Monday morning, we all
would come in and we would actually talk

00:17:15.275 --> 00:17:19.820
about what we read and what were the good
ideas, and who were the great writers.

00:17:19.820 --> 00:17:22.284
And then you would move
through the day and

00:17:22.284 --> 00:17:25.736
there would be things about
meeting with filmmakers, and

00:17:25.736 --> 00:17:30.300
hearing what their take on their
story was, why they wanted to do it.

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:35.390
And then kind of through the process
of a movie of everything

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:40.720
from assembling the team around it,
the producers,

00:17:40.720 --> 00:17:45.300
the directors, the casts,
pretty much every element of it.

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:48.090
Where was it going to be done,
the budget, the marketing.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:52.643
So these are all elements of movie making.

00:17:52.643 --> 00:17:55.181
And they're still kind
of relevant today and

00:17:55.181 --> 00:17:58.470
it's probably organize a little
bit differently today.

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:02.338
But, certainly back then,
that was what a day was for me.

00:18:02.338 --> 00:18:07.321
And we have previews of our movies,
we always found a great collaboration with

00:18:07.321 --> 00:18:11.410
our audience when we'd have a movie
that was a work in progress.

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:17.892
Having that moment where a filmmaker or
a storyteller could actually

00:18:17.892 --> 00:18:23.946
hear an audience in that sort of just
natural involuntary response to a film and

00:18:23.946 --> 00:18:29.383
to see was it going too fast,
was it going too slow, was it confusing?

00:18:29.383 --> 00:18:33.555
That's just just sitting in the back of an
audience, it's not about sort of testing,

00:18:33.555 --> 00:18:37.614
it's not about sort of filling out preview
cards which I'm sure everybody is done,

00:18:37.614 --> 00:18:39.060
that stuff kind of nonsense.

00:18:39.060 --> 00:18:41.404
But just sitting in
an audience you can tell so

00:18:41.404 --> 00:18:44.930
much about things that are working and
things that are not working.

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:51.330
&gt;&gt; You've described your work ethic and
giving all Saturday,

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:54.160
part of Sunday, making work your life.

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:57.410
Have you seen that have a really
strong impact on the teams around you?

00:18:57.410 --> 00:18:59.300
How has that evolved for you?

00:18:59.300 --> 00:19:04.569
&gt;&gt; Well, it's fortunate of all, because I
started off sort of I guess neanderthal,

00:19:04.569 --> 00:19:07.806
because I actually once
said a number of years ago,

00:19:07.806 --> 00:19:12.422
if you don't come to work on Saturday,
don't bother coming on Sunday.

00:19:12.422 --> 00:19:19.318
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now, here's the problem with that.

00:19:19.318 --> 00:19:22.108
When I said it I meant it.

00:19:22.108 --> 00:19:27.468
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:19:27.468 --> 00:19:31.340
the lessons that I learned, I think,

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:36.785
anybody learns is that you
learn that working smarter

00:19:36.785 --> 00:19:41.746
sometimes has as much value
as working harder And

00:19:41.746 --> 00:19:46.949
you also learn that having
what is sort of popularly

00:19:46.949 --> 00:19:55.320
referred to as sort of work-life balance
is not just a nice theoretical thought.

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:57.591
I think it's actually true.

00:19:57.591 --> 00:20:04.646
And so I can kid all I want about it, but
the fact is I have a wonderful family,

00:20:04.646 --> 00:20:09.165
I have been married for
45 years to an amazing,

00:20:09.165 --> 00:20:13.709
amazing partner, have 2 kids, 3 grandkids.

00:20:16.510 --> 00:20:21.496
Very proud of how my kids grew up and
how they became people,

00:20:21.496 --> 00:20:25.906
and found their own identity and
their own careers.

00:20:25.906 --> 00:20:32.790
And that became an important part of it,
and so in my 20s, that was probably fine.

00:20:32.790 --> 00:20:34.130
It's probably fine for all of you.

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:37.990
But there's a moment in time in which you
actually have to find those things that

00:20:37.990 --> 00:20:42.420
make you whole as a human being and
then make you better at what you're doing.

00:20:42.420 --> 00:20:48.701
And so having that element as
an important part of your life is,

00:20:48.701 --> 00:20:53.806
I think,
an invaluable asset of doing a great job.

00:20:53.806 --> 00:20:56.974
&gt;&gt; As the boss respecting that in
your team, in the team around you and

00:20:56.974 --> 00:20:58.220
what you're building.

00:20:58.220 --> 00:20:59.430
&gt;&gt; Have to, yeah.

00:21:01.030 --> 00:21:07.000
&gt;&gt; So if we move into he Disney years,
everyone has a favorite child.

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:11.176
So do you have a favorite
film from that time?

00:21:11.176 --> 00:21:15.984
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There are different

00:21:15.984 --> 00:21:20.034
movies that have different.

00:21:20.034 --> 00:21:25.151
So, first of all,
the other genetic defect I have and

00:21:25.151 --> 00:21:32.900
addition to those others that I gave you
is that I don't have a reminiscent gene.

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:37.360
And so, I tend not to look back.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:40.290
And most of all when
something's finished for

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:42.810
me I turn the chapter and
I go to the next thing.

00:21:42.810 --> 00:21:45.940
And so, at least to date, I've not yet

00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:52.150
found that moment to turn back and
actually be reflective in a way about it.

00:21:52.150 --> 00:21:57.080
But there are things and stories
that I have told over the years that

00:21:58.270 --> 00:22:05.270
had a connection to me, because I had
something to do with the idea itself,

00:22:05.270 --> 00:22:10.360
and the idea was in some way a reflection
of something going on for me.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:16.858
I say that and I want to wave the flag
of saying, I had some peace in that,

00:22:16.858 --> 00:22:22.036
because I don't want to take
credit from great writers,

00:22:22.036 --> 00:22:28.556
great directors, great filmmakers
whose names are on these things.

00:22:28.556 --> 00:22:31.757
Sam Goldwyn who is one of
the great producers in

00:22:31.757 --> 00:22:35.124
early Hollywood had
an expression which I love,

00:22:35.124 --> 00:22:39.489
which he said, credit you give
yourself isn't worth having.

00:22:39.489 --> 00:22:43.170
And so, I say that as a caveat here of,

00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:48.347
the story of the Lion King
is a reflection of something

00:22:48.347 --> 00:22:52.760
that had happen to me in my life,
in my 20s.

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:57.510
And I wanted to tell that in a story,
not the Hamlet part.

00:22:57.510 --> 00:22:59.224
That had nothing to do with.

00:22:59.224 --> 00:23:03.319
[LAUGH] There's a guy, Willy Shake-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:23:03.319 --> 00:23:05.200
&gt;&gt; Something or rather, he was that.

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:10.816
But the notion that for
those of who may have a minor

00:23:10.816 --> 00:23:16.166
memory of this is that
Simba believes that he was

00:23:16.166 --> 00:23:21.649
responsible for
the death of his dad and he runs,

00:23:21.649 --> 00:23:27.267
because he does not feel
he can confront that truth

00:23:27.267 --> 00:23:32.535
to his mom and to his family,
and to his tribe.

00:23:32.535 --> 00:23:37.870
And so he goes away and finds Pumba and
Timon and Hakuna Makata.

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:44.593
And eventually,
Nala shows up, this childhood

00:23:44.593 --> 00:23:50.039
friend of his, and
reminds him of what's there and

00:23:50.039 --> 00:23:54.980
then between Rafiki
shaking his magic wand and

00:23:54.980 --> 00:23:59.304
Mufasa from up there, you must return.

00:23:59.304 --> 00:24:02.794
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He goes back and

00:24:02.794 --> 00:24:08.600
faces his truth And
I had a situation in my life and

00:24:08.600 --> 00:24:13.120
my career in which that happened to me,
not in the familiar way,

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:17.580
which I felt would be the way in
which we could all relate to it.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:22.310
But there was a set of circumstances
in which everything was on the line for

00:24:22.310 --> 00:24:25.580
me and it was a chance for
me to cut and run.

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:30.099
And I knew ultimately someday,
you cannot run from the truth,

00:24:30.099 --> 00:24:31.898
the truth will find you.

00:24:31.898 --> 00:24:36.266
And so, I made a very bold and
very hard and very difficult decision for

00:24:36.266 --> 00:24:41.005
me to come back and face the truth and by
the way the outcome was great for me and

00:24:41.005 --> 00:24:45.004
doing that and I just felt that was
such a life learning lesson for

00:24:45.004 --> 00:24:47.927
me that I wanted to try and
tell that in a story.

00:24:47.927 --> 00:24:54.499
And another movie I've made which
probably many of you don't ever know or

00:24:54.499 --> 00:25:00.859
mabye barely heard about, which was
also a reflection of something for

00:25:00.859 --> 00:25:07.970
me, which is about how I feel about life,
I actually don't like the word, no.

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:12.856
Maybe some of you do but
that doesn't work for

00:25:12.856 --> 00:25:17.999
me, in pretty much any and
so, there was a movie

00:25:17.999 --> 00:25:23.015
called Spirits,
Stallion of the Cimarron and

00:25:23.015 --> 00:25:28.800
it was the last hand drawn
animated movie that I made,

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:33.556
as that sort of revolution
of CG came along,

00:25:33.556 --> 00:25:37.184
Struck was the next one after it.

00:25:37.184 --> 00:25:43.730
And it was a very, very,
it was my homage to really,

00:25:43.730 --> 00:25:49.322
one of the great things that
I think Walt Disney did,

00:25:49.322 --> 00:25:54.000
which was, it's to allow

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:59.430
animation to be it's best and

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:03.910
to let the pictures do
all the storytelling.

00:26:03.910 --> 00:26:10.370
So, the lead character,
which is this indomitable character,

00:26:10.370 --> 00:26:15.950
Spirit, he doesn't talk, right?

00:26:15.950 --> 00:26:21.120
So there's, it relies on animation
to actually tell itself and

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:23.740
it is a musical and nobody sings,

00:26:23.740 --> 00:26:28.230
other than Bryan Adams who sings
kind of sound track to it.

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:32.125
So it was a really incredibly ambitious,

00:26:32.125 --> 00:26:35.942
very risky animated movie that failed.

00:26:35.942 --> 00:26:40.600
[LAUGH] But not for me and
that story specifically of

00:26:40.600 --> 00:26:46.300
Spirit which is, he is a character
that no matter what the circumstances,

00:26:46.300 --> 00:26:51.214
his optimism and his determination
would not allow him to fail.

00:26:51.214 --> 00:26:58.988
And that's me, I just have
a bottomless well of optimism and,

00:26:58.988 --> 00:27:04.122
so and
then the third one is I grew up across

00:27:04.122 --> 00:27:09.130
from the New York City Central Park Zoo.

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:13.520
And I used to go there as a young kid
five, six, seven, eight years old and

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:20.150
when I would see the zoo
through my eyes as

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:24.350
a kid, I thought well,
they live on Fifth Avenue.

00:27:24.350 --> 00:27:27.030
I'd see they got steak everyday.

00:27:27.030 --> 00:27:30.740
They were groomed and taken care of and
their cages were already washed and

00:27:30.740 --> 00:27:32.350
cleaned everyday and I thought, God.

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:35.220
These animals,
they're living the top of the line and

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:38.870
I thought well, what would happen if
they had to actually go back to Africa?

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:41.280
And there is Madagascar.

00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:47.630
&gt;&gt; It sounds like this incredibly
personal connections for

00:27:47.630 --> 00:27:51.280
you in making these films,
the ones that really stick with you.

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:56.083
&gt;&gt; Well, the animated ones for sure but
they're great movies over the years that

00:27:56.083 --> 00:28:00.742
are just wonderful things were great,
film makers, great storytellers,

00:28:00.742 --> 00:28:04.624
great artists have these amazing
stories they wanted to tell.

00:28:04.624 --> 00:28:07.768
And just to kind of be on
the sideline cheerleading them,

00:28:07.768 --> 00:28:11.055
helping them realize those
things was just very exciting.

00:28:11.055 --> 00:28:15.903
But I made 406 live action movies,
when I say I made,

00:28:15.903 --> 00:28:20.959
they were made while I was,
overseeing these studios over

00:28:20.959 --> 00:28:26.270
the years and 41 animated movies and
there's a lot.

00:28:26.270 --> 00:28:29.579
And man,
there's just a ton of dogs in there.

00:28:29.579 --> 00:28:34.590
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] You had an abrupt departure

00:28:34.590 --> 00:28:39.400
from Disney, can you talk to
us about what that was like?

00:28:39.400 --> 00:28:44.370
&gt;&gt; Sure, so in the Summer of 1994,

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:50.900
the number one movie in
the world was the Lion King.

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:57.780
The number one Album, soundtrack or

00:28:57.780 --> 00:29:02.280
actually record, period,
was soundtrack to Lion King,

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:05.900
sold 27, 28 million hard copies.

00:29:05.900 --> 00:29:09.715
Anybody want to think about that today?

00:29:09.715 --> 00:29:12.111
[LAUGH] How extraordinary that is.

00:29:14.259 --> 00:29:18.570
The number one home video in the world
was from Aladdin, the year before.

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:24.460
The number one show on Broadway
was Beauty and the Beast.

00:29:24.460 --> 00:29:28.020
The number one TV show was a show we
produced called Home Improvement.

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:31.045
The number one book on
the New York Times Best, you get the idea.

00:29:31.045 --> 00:29:35.690
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Like at a moment in time and

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:39.250
I don't know whether that's
happened again since or not,

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:43.150
maybe it has, certainly possible for
the Disney Company,

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:48.770
we had number 1 of every single place
that we produced for, which is pretty

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:53.880
much across the entire spectrum of things
that are around entertainment and culture.

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:55.568
I got fired.

00:29:55.568 --> 00:30:02.880
And it was horribly ugly.

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:06.840
I mean, it was, I had,

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:11.760
we've been together with
the CEO of the company.

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:15.984
Michael Eisner and I, we're partners
together for 18 and a half years and

00:30:15.984 --> 00:30:19.284
I guess the analogy I would
make except I'm fortunately and

00:30:19.284 --> 00:30:22.460
I have not experienced this but
I have friends that have.

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:25.870
It's like a marriage when perfectly
two appoint and then somehow or

00:30:25.870 --> 00:30:30.630
another just got on the line and there is
a reason for this, is that Michael and

00:30:30.630 --> 00:30:35.950
I had this amazing marriage counselor
who was the president of the company,

00:30:35.950 --> 00:30:39.460
amazing guy, selfless, one of the most
selfless people I've ever met,

00:30:39.460 --> 00:30:41.080
his name is Frank Wells.

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:44.790
Ans so, there was Michael as
the CEO of the company and

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:49.976
Frank was the COO and
I was the CEO of the Walt Disney Studios.

00:30:49.976 --> 00:30:55.480
And Frank, [COUGH] for
all of these years, 10 years,

00:30:55.480 --> 00:31:00.390
he was the guy that always kind of made
things right between Michael and I.

00:31:00.390 --> 00:31:03.200
And that when we would
sort of get up on the line or

00:31:03.200 --> 00:31:06.760
at each other or I wasn't being sensitive
or not, for not being inclusive,

00:31:06.760 --> 00:31:09.810
whatever those things are, Frank
would come in my office, sit down and

00:31:09.810 --> 00:31:13.850
put his feet up on the table and go, I
think there's a better way of doing this.

00:31:13.850 --> 00:31:17.730
And he managed to do that and
then quite tragically, in April of 1994,

00:31:17.730 --> 00:31:20.320
he died in a helicopter crash.

00:31:21.860 --> 00:31:26.090
This is where one of my partners
always said to me Jeffery,

00:31:26.090 --> 00:31:29.800
there's your plan and there's God's
plan and your plan doesn't count.

00:31:29.800 --> 00:31:35.370
And this is one of those moments where
suddenly everything that worked,

00:31:35.370 --> 00:31:37.070
nothing worked.

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:42.020
And so Michael and
I got sideways with one another,

00:31:42.020 --> 00:31:47.570
very quickly and
they way to became this amazing eruption.

00:31:49.660 --> 00:31:53.940
And he fired me and
at the moment in time in which I

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:57.490
wasn't doing my job as well
as I had ever done it before.

00:31:57.490 --> 00:32:03.250
And at the time, it was incredibly
public and incredibly ugly and

00:32:03.250 --> 00:32:09.200
very personal and very nasty and it
resulted in a lawsuit and Just went on for

00:32:09.200 --> 00:32:14.920
a really long time, and I would have
to rank that as probably my greatest

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:19.845
regret professionally, not that I
got fired, it's how I got fired.

00:32:19.845 --> 00:32:25.100
And that may not make any sense to you,
but I just look

00:32:25.100 --> 00:32:30.040
back today and there must've been a better
way for me to go about separating.

00:32:31.720 --> 00:32:38.680
And if I could have a do over,
at least I would try and do that.

00:32:38.680 --> 00:32:42.080
And I don't know that it would
have a different outcome.

00:32:42.080 --> 00:32:42.970
I don't think it would, but

00:32:42.970 --> 00:32:45.670
it would have had a better way
to get to the outcome of it.

00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:51.690
because it's the most humiliating,
embarrassing moment of my career.

00:32:51.690 --> 00:32:56.890
I felt just completely demeaned

00:32:56.890 --> 00:33:00.941
by it and just hurt by it.

00:33:00.941 --> 00:33:01.696
And it was hard.

00:33:01.696 --> 00:33:06.481
Eight days later after getting fired,
I started DreamWorks with

00:33:06.481 --> 00:33:11.205
David Geffen [LAUGH] so I got over
it in about seven and a half days.

00:33:11.205 --> 00:33:15.338
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Pretty quick turnaround.

00:33:15.338 --> 00:33:17.350
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:22.890
&gt;&gt; But I just hear so much love for the
films and the people involved at Disney,

00:33:22.890 --> 00:33:26.580
and I really appreciate that
you shared all of that with us.

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:27.440
But you did,

00:33:27.440 --> 00:33:31.380
you turned right around and took
an entrepreneurial leap when you could've

00:33:31.380 --> 00:33:35.420
taken an operating role at any of the
established studios with the track record.

00:33:35.420 --> 00:33:37.930
I mean, you'd just made
number one on every measure.

00:33:37.930 --> 00:33:42.870
So why take the leap?

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:47.679
&gt;&gt; Well, again,
the sort of recurring thing for me and

00:33:47.679 --> 00:33:52.162
literally going back to
those days of Lindsay and

00:33:52.162 --> 00:33:55.881
Paramount and Disney and DreamWorks and

00:33:55.881 --> 00:34:01.480
again here today now,
I have loved the idea of a blank slate.

00:34:01.480 --> 00:34:06.350
I know from most people
it's a scary thing,

00:34:06.350 --> 00:34:12.812
particularly, and
most of these almost like clockwork.

00:34:12.812 --> 00:34:15.179
These have been ten-year
chapters in my career.

00:34:15.179 --> 00:34:20.154
And so they're long and they're deep
roots and it's attached to people and

00:34:20.154 --> 00:34:25.340
projects and friends and family that I
grew up within each of these chapters.

00:34:25.340 --> 00:34:29.600
But I find that for me,

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:33.620
whether it's the circumstances changed,
the team broke up, or

00:34:33.620 --> 00:34:39.300
I got fired, starting over has
always been fantastic for me.

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:43.830
And what has come in front of me
has always, every single time,

00:34:43.830 --> 00:34:46.720
no matter how great the circumstances, so

00:34:46.720 --> 00:34:51.440
as great as Paramount was for
me, or Disney was for me.

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:55.920
And they were fantastic times and
incredible decades there.

00:34:55.920 --> 00:35:00.670
And so much to love and be proud of,
what came next was better.

00:35:00.670 --> 00:35:06.190
And so when Comcast showed up a year and
a half ago,

00:35:06.190 --> 00:35:11.550
I thought my third act was going to be
doing another ten years of DreamWorks,

00:35:11.550 --> 00:35:13.865
and making number 407 and 42.

00:35:13.865 --> 00:35:17.820
[LAUGH] And at first it was like,

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:21.170
what do you mean, you want my company,
you don't want me?

00:35:21.170 --> 00:35:23.613
It was like, well,
I'm kind of hurt by that for a moment.

00:35:23.613 --> 00:35:28.173
And then I thought about it, and honestly
one day, I woke up the next morning and

00:35:28.173 --> 00:35:30.300
went, no, no, no, wait a minute.

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:34.466
This is a chance to be 25 years old again,
with extraordinary,

00:35:34.466 --> 00:35:38.576
extraordinary resources and
contacts and all of these things.

00:35:38.576 --> 00:35:46.260
And honestly, I never imagined there would
be another get to start all over again.

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:52.170
And the moment that that idea just came
into my head, I couldn't get it out.

00:35:52.170 --> 00:35:54.990
I couldn't get away, and
it became more exciting to me.

00:35:54.990 --> 00:35:59.990
And so
passing the baton to the NBC Universal

00:35:59.990 --> 00:36:04.470
people on DreamWorks seemed like
the most obvious must do thing.

00:36:04.470 --> 00:36:07.943
It was better for my employees because
they would be part of this $200 billion

00:36:07.943 --> 00:36:10.453
company, have more job security and
more resources.

00:36:10.453 --> 00:36:13.295
And these are people that have
been with me a long time and

00:36:13.295 --> 00:36:16.380
I felt a great sense of obligation and
loyalty to them.

00:36:16.380 --> 00:36:20.730
I could afford to take risks,
they might not.

00:36:20.730 --> 00:36:24.976
And all of these characters in movies and
IP that we had created,

00:36:24.976 --> 00:36:29.926
to put them in the hands of people that
could made them live forever if they so

00:36:29.926 --> 00:36:35.376
choose to, there couldn't be a better
place or a better organization to do that.

00:36:35.376 --> 00:36:40.130
And for the shareholders,
it was an extraordinary outcome.

00:36:40.130 --> 00:36:43.840
Stock was selling at $21 a share,
they bought it for $41 a share.

00:36:43.840 --> 00:36:48.390
And so I couldn't put myself in

00:36:48.390 --> 00:36:52.640
the way of all of those three outcomes
which were better for everybody.

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:55.430
And then once that idea of, okay,
you can start all over again.

00:36:55.430 --> 00:36:57.202
I went, touchdown, let's go.

00:36:57.202 --> 00:37:01.040
&gt;&gt; Made it.

00:37:01.040 --> 00:37:06.370
So you are looking into the future
with excitement in your new venture.

00:37:06.370 --> 00:37:09.450
You are looking for
the team to pull around you.

00:37:09.450 --> 00:37:13.250
And before we were talking,
you described building a team for

00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:15.790
a business as a casting process.

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:20.022
So how are you casting for
your new venture right now?

00:37:20.022 --> 00:37:22.489
&gt;&gt; Well, again,
one of the recurring things for

00:37:22.489 --> 00:37:26.852
me over the years is I've always had great
mentors as I talked about Barry Diller,

00:37:26.852 --> 00:37:28.980
but Michael Eisner was a great mentor.

00:37:28.980 --> 00:37:31.760
Again, we were together for
18 and a half of those years.

00:37:31.760 --> 00:37:36.990
It was a phenomenal partnership and
I learned so much from him.

00:37:36.990 --> 00:37:41.610
And so I've had a series of
amazing mentors over the years.

00:37:41.610 --> 00:37:44.779
Two people who were mentors that
went on to do something different.

00:37:44.779 --> 00:37:46.901
Well, one, Barry Diller, and

00:37:46.901 --> 00:37:51.578
he started this company called
IAC about 25 years ago.

00:37:51.578 --> 00:37:54.730
And the other was John Malone and
Liberty Media.

00:37:54.730 --> 00:37:59.350
And I saw in what they did
something that excited me,

00:37:59.350 --> 00:38:02.680
because I'm a builder,
and I'm an operator.

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:05.460
I'm not an investor.

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:09.475
And I believe that the most
valuable thing is human capital.

00:38:09.475 --> 00:38:13.416
To build businesses, to do things,

00:38:13.416 --> 00:38:18.640
obviously, you need the cash
to be able to do it.

00:38:18.640 --> 00:38:21.230
But the most valuable
thing is human capital.

00:38:21.230 --> 00:38:27.620
And so, in starting WonderCo I
wanted to put together a partnership

00:38:27.620 --> 00:38:33.540
of people that if the day you
were going to have a business,

00:38:33.540 --> 00:38:36.856
either one that you were starting or
one that you already had started.

00:38:36.856 --> 00:38:41.841
And you wanted exceptional
resources to help you take

00:38:41.841 --> 00:38:46.826
that to some great new plateau,
that within WonderCo

00:38:46.826 --> 00:38:51.937
we actually had everything
you would want in a C suite.

00:38:51.937 --> 00:38:56.271
You have a great strategist,
you have a great marketing executive,

00:38:56.271 --> 00:38:59.580
you have a general counsel,
a CFO, a COO, a CEO.

00:38:59.580 --> 00:39:06.570
Everything that you would want in
a place like that, we got some of.

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:09.780
And so to have that as sort of
the resource because when I look at

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:12.600
Barry Diller and John Malone,
that's what they have done.

00:39:12.600 --> 00:39:17.740
They had found companies around ideas and
around entrepreneurs, and

00:39:17.740 --> 00:39:25.030
then they have put a ton of people
capital into those enterprises.

00:39:25.030 --> 00:39:27.320
Diller started IAC 25 years ago.

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:31.523
His idea was that intersection
of the Internet and

00:39:31.523 --> 00:39:36.740
to be able to create commercial
consumer transactions on it.

00:39:36.740 --> 00:39:40.890
So everything from Expedia to I'm sure no
one in this room has ever actually done

00:39:40.890 --> 00:39:44.102
anything that has anything
to do with Match.com.

00:39:44.102 --> 00:39:48.090
[LAUGH]
So

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:52.480
Barry's probably built literally $70,
80, 90 billion worth of market cap.

00:39:52.480 --> 00:39:56.250
I mean, it's like a quiet story,
not maybe that well told.

00:39:56.250 --> 00:39:58.780
But he's literally one of the greatest
entrepreneurs of our time.

00:39:58.780 --> 00:40:03.470
And I think right now, today,
is exactly another one of those moments.

00:40:03.470 --> 00:40:08.410
And today that intersection is technology.

00:40:08.410 --> 00:40:11.840
And the intersection of video.

00:40:11.840 --> 00:40:16.730
And the application of video against,
or integrated with technology.

00:40:16.730 --> 00:40:21.530
And how transformative and
imperative that is going to be for

00:40:21.530 --> 00:40:29.648
what I think is the next evolutionary, if
not revolutionary part of our world today.

00:40:29.648 --> 00:40:32.985
I'll try and maybe translate that,

00:40:32.985 --> 00:40:37.720
which is if you go back maybe,
I don't know, 12,

00:40:37.720 --> 00:40:43.012
14 years ago,
everybody was on a thing called a Nextel.

00:40:43.012 --> 00:40:44.017
Most people in this room were, too.

00:40:44.017 --> 00:40:47.890
This is you could send a text to somebody
on this little sort of pager device.

00:40:47.890 --> 00:40:50.612
Somebody up here laughed,
it's probably over 40.

00:40:50.612 --> 00:40:51.781
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:40:51.781 --> 00:40:56.040
then we went from that to a flip phone.

00:40:56.040 --> 00:40:59.040
And you had to actually hit
three times to get an R.

00:40:59.040 --> 00:41:03.225
I know this,
I'm speaking Swahili to you but trust me.

00:41:03.225 --> 00:41:03.880
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:41:03.880 --> 00:41:06.270
then I'm now getting into territory here.

00:41:06.270 --> 00:41:10.110
We went to a Blackberry, and
then from a Blackberry to a smartphone.

00:41:10.110 --> 00:41:14.300
And so in a course of about 12, 14 years,

00:41:14.300 --> 00:41:20.100
everything around text, the application
of text, the platforms around text.

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:25.009
So if you think about communication,
collaboration, sharing, search,

00:41:25.009 --> 00:41:28.140
e-commerce and even entertainment.

00:41:28.140 --> 00:41:33.141
In a very short handful of years,
text became ubiquitous,

00:41:33.141 --> 00:41:38.043
the enterprise is built around
text became worth hundreds

00:41:38.043 --> 00:41:41.559
of billions if not trillions of dollars.

00:41:41.559 --> 00:41:45.180
And all of us became expert at it.

00:41:45.180 --> 00:41:49.190
So, literally the ability of all of us and
any of us and

00:41:49.190 --> 00:41:54.630
anyone of us to use text against
those seven verticals today,

00:41:54.630 --> 00:41:58.340
is honestly as easy as putting
one foot in front of another.

00:41:58.340 --> 00:42:01.200
So then about five, six years after that

00:42:01.200 --> 00:42:06.180
happen the next thing that happen in
that sort of evolution was photography.

00:42:06.180 --> 00:42:12.490
And so today every single one of us
is like a junior and Annie Liebowitz.

00:42:12.490 --> 00:42:18.260
We can all take great photos no matter
how inept we are we can't help but

00:42:18.260 --> 00:42:20.060
take great photos.

00:42:20.060 --> 00:42:22.683
And so now think about
these in these last four or

00:42:22.683 --> 00:42:25.710
five years now,
the impact that photography has had.

00:42:25.710 --> 00:42:27.040
So we had moved.

00:42:27.040 --> 00:42:30.300
And it's not displaced text,
it's added on text.

00:42:30.300 --> 00:42:31.100
Why?

00:42:31.100 --> 00:42:34.420
Because a picture is a thousand,
is better than a thousand words.

00:42:34.420 --> 00:42:36.090
You've heard that said before on it.

00:42:36.090 --> 00:42:39.685
So, now you think about communication,
collaboration, you get it.

00:42:39.685 --> 00:42:44.410
[LAUGH] The application of
photography has been transformed and

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:48.240
once again many hundreds and
billions of dollars of market values.

00:42:48.240 --> 00:42:52.104
Whether it's Instagram or Pinterest and
we can go on and on, okay?

00:42:52.104 --> 00:42:54.390
So we're now in the third cycle.

00:42:54.390 --> 00:42:58.610
The sort of next third evolution
is the application of video

00:42:58.610 --> 00:43:02.068
against each of those
very specific verticals.

00:43:02.068 --> 00:43:06.223
And you're seeing what happens
when people come along and

00:43:06.223 --> 00:43:11.067
come up with the application of
putting these two things together.

00:43:11.067 --> 00:43:14.930
So two perfect examples
of it are Snapchat right?

00:43:14.930 --> 00:43:17.090
In the world of communication and sharing.

00:43:17.090 --> 00:43:21.512
If you thinking of an entertainment
best of it is Netflix.

00:43:21.512 --> 00:43:25.559
Netflix is people forget it, they were
again I don't know if anybody remember,

00:43:25.559 --> 00:43:27.950
they used to have a red
envelope with a DVD in it.

00:43:27.950 --> 00:43:30.682
That's only seven years ago.

00:43:30.682 --> 00:43:35.381
Now, it's streaming and
a $100 billion market

00:43:35.381 --> 00:43:40.100
cap over the intersection of video and
technology.

00:43:40.100 --> 00:43:41.780
So for is today,

00:43:41.780 --> 00:43:46.292
we have a team that we have build
that we think bridges that world.

00:43:46.292 --> 00:43:50.180
And there's 12 of us,

00:43:50.180 --> 00:43:54.605
9 of which came were graduates of
Stanford, so thank you all very much.

00:43:54.605 --> 00:43:57.090
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; One

00:43:57.090 --> 00:43:59.560
of us is not a graduate of Stanford.

00:43:59.560 --> 00:44:03.102
He couldn't get in here, so he went
to Harvard Business School instead.

00:44:03.102 --> 00:44:05.458
But he teaches here,
which is Sujay Jaswajoi.

00:44:05.458 --> 00:44:07.896
He should stand up and
wave hello there because hopefully,

00:44:07.896 --> 00:44:09.760
some of you are being taught by him today.

00:44:09.760 --> 00:44:12.200
Come on Sujay, show that t-shirt off.

00:44:12.200 --> 00:44:15.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Stand up, stand up.

00:44:15.140 --> 00:44:17.225
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There we go.

00:44:17.225 --> 00:44:22.138
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And so

00:44:22.138 --> 00:44:26.163
we have a partnership that I
think is a great marriage and

00:44:26.163 --> 00:44:31.980
collaboration between Northern California
and Southern California.

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:36.419
Which right now at this moment in time is
highly unique because we are different

00:44:36.419 --> 00:44:39.458
cultures, we actually
speak different languages.

00:44:39.458 --> 00:44:44.308
But our co-dependency on one another,
the integration of these two

00:44:44.308 --> 00:44:49.500
worlds is literally these two trains
that are on their way at one another.

00:44:49.500 --> 00:44:54.460
And so for Wonder Co we believe
we have the human capital

00:44:54.460 --> 00:44:58.509
to facilitate that and to take advantage
of it and to be entrepreneurial around it.

00:44:59.630 --> 00:45:03.960
&gt;&gt; An incredible vision, you seem to
have pretty boundless ambition for

00:45:03.960 --> 00:45:04.950
what's to come and

00:45:04.950 --> 00:45:08.190
Meg Whitman is obviously going to blend
a huge amount of experience for that.

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:11.080
Well, it's a perfect example,
I've known Meg for 30 years.

00:45:11.080 --> 00:45:18.170
She's truly one of the most extraordinary
leaders and just great people.

00:45:18.170 --> 00:45:20.160
She was on my board at
DreamWorks Animation.

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:22.060
We met when I was at Disney.

00:45:22.060 --> 00:45:23.840
We've started this new business,

00:45:23.840 --> 00:45:28.080
which is right now we as our
working title is called New TV.

00:45:28.080 --> 00:45:33.010
But it's an incredibly, incredibly
exciting, ambitious, improbable,

00:45:33.010 --> 00:45:39.390
likely impossible dream that she and
I now are sharing.

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:45.347
And it can only work by putting
our two worlds together,

00:45:45.347 --> 00:45:51.195
and she brings just a level
of knowledge of expertise.

00:45:51.195 --> 00:45:57.340
She's been at every, whether it's what
she did at eBay or certainly at HP.

00:45:57.340 --> 00:46:02.950
She just has an incredible,
incredible set of skills I don't.

00:46:02.950 --> 00:46:07.532
And it is,
I believe it is one of those things where

00:46:07.532 --> 00:46:10.377
1 plus 1 is going to equal 11.

00:46:10.377 --> 00:46:11.120
&gt;&gt; Better to see it.

00:46:11.120 --> 00:46:14.100
I want to thank you for
everything that you shared with us so far.

00:46:14.100 --> 00:46:16.292
We're going to pause for
a moment to open up to questions.

00:46:16.292 --> 00:46:17.096
&gt;&gt; Love it.

00:46:17.096 --> 00:46:18.530
&gt;&gt; And than we'll close.

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:19.030
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:46:26.448 --> 00:46:29.340
All right, my name is David,
and I'm a second year here.

00:46:29.340 --> 00:46:33.742
And I'm inspired by your talk of
the movement from the third, to second,

00:46:33.742 --> 00:46:35.170
to third wave of video.

00:46:35.170 --> 00:46:39.040
Does Wonder Co have any sort of ambitions
on saying what's after the third wave?

00:46:40.560 --> 00:46:42.552
Well no,
because we've got to get to the third.

00:46:42.552 --> 00:46:47.870
[LAUGH] I think we're at the beginning
of the beginning of the third wave,

00:46:47.870 --> 00:46:55.430
but I actually think that you just
imagine what the application is.

00:46:55.430 --> 00:47:00.560
Let me give you just one place here,
which is e-commerce.

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:04.740
So think of how much E-Commerce
was changed from text to photo.

00:47:04.740 --> 00:47:09.850
Right, that the ability that you
can actually look at commerce on

00:47:09.850 --> 00:47:15.030
your phone and see displayed for you.

00:47:15.030 --> 00:47:18.550
Every single version sneaker
you can imagine, right?

00:47:18.550 --> 00:47:20.130
Okay now, move to video.

00:47:20.130 --> 00:47:25.254
And video, which is how the human being
actually functions, sight and sound.

00:47:25.254 --> 00:47:29.237
And it is really the way in which we
ingest data in the most effective,

00:47:29.237 --> 00:47:33.310
most efficient way and
we can put emotions around those things.

00:47:33.310 --> 00:47:36.915
So now think about how all those versions
of Nike will look like in video.

00:47:36.915 --> 00:47:40.639
So and I say that because we're in Mr.

00:47:40.639 --> 00:47:43.568
Nike's home here,
&gt;&gt; Building.

00:47:43.568 --> 00:47:46.364
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So you can thank him for this.

00:47:46.364 --> 00:47:53.250
So but, the application of video for
commerce is going to be transformative.

00:47:53.250 --> 00:47:58.124
As much as we think we had a,
I would say we're in that sort of

00:47:58.124 --> 00:48:03.794
evolutionary revolutionary moment
from retail into E-commerce.

00:48:03.794 --> 00:48:06.444
I would bet that we'll look at five,
seven years from now,

00:48:06.444 --> 00:48:10.630
it won't look anything like, I mean,
it'll just go to a whole another level.

00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:12.360
By the application of video.

00:48:12.360 --> 00:48:14.398
Just that one, and
now we can go to the other verticals and

00:48:14.398 --> 00:48:15.559
I think it'll be as impactful.

00:48:19.228 --> 00:48:19.786
&gt;&gt; I'm Arial.

00:48:19.786 --> 00:48:22.528
I don’t know if this is on.

00:48:22.528 --> 00:48:23.202
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we can hear you.

00:48:23.202 --> 00:48:28.142
&gt;&gt; Great, I'm an MBA I and my question is
what you learned from your partnership

00:48:28.142 --> 00:48:33.386
with Spielberg and Geffen and how you've
applied that into building Wonder Co and

00:48:33.386 --> 00:48:36.967
how you'll build you future
teams within the company.

00:48:36.967 --> 00:48:41.806
&gt;&gt; Well,
I mean it's amazing partnership and

00:48:41.806 --> 00:48:49.230
I think taught me about what
the value of partnering can be.

00:48:49.230 --> 00:48:51.930
When Stephen and Dave and
I came together in 1994.

00:48:51.930 --> 00:48:56.043
If you went to Las Vegas, probably
as many of you are going to tonight,

00:48:56.043 --> 00:49:00.015
you could place a bet on how long would
the three of them be able to be in

00:49:00.015 --> 00:49:02.765
the same sandbox without
killing each other.

00:49:02.765 --> 00:49:10.126
Right, and the odds were 100 to 1
that it would blow up in a year or

00:49:10.126 --> 00:49:14.258
two [COUGH] but what Steven and David and

00:49:14.258 --> 00:49:20.350
I found in each other [COUGH]
is very specific expertise.

00:49:20.350 --> 00:49:24.150
And as partners we actually
could rely on one another.

00:49:24.150 --> 00:49:27.320
So quite obviously in
the world of story telling,

00:49:30.060 --> 00:49:33.080
Steven Spielberg arguably is
the greatest storyteller,

00:49:33.080 --> 00:49:36.650
certainly among greatest
storytellers of our time.

00:49:36.650 --> 00:49:41.920
And so
to defer to him as the sort of final

00:49:41.920 --> 00:49:47.340
arbiter in that regard seems easy and
good to do, and even for

00:49:47.340 --> 00:49:53.900
me who had had amazing success
before we came to that partnership.

00:49:53.900 --> 00:49:56.183
I always felt deferential.

00:49:56.183 --> 00:50:00.453
And even on our animated movies,
I would always bring Steven in,

00:50:00.453 --> 00:50:02.640
I mean, right up to the very end.

00:50:02.640 --> 00:50:05.590
Once a week, he'd come over and
give me a morning.

00:50:05.590 --> 00:50:08.160
And I would engage him in every way and
any way.

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:10.090
I didn't always agree with him, but

00:50:10.090 --> 00:50:14.390
I always wanted to hear
what he would have to say.

00:50:14.390 --> 00:50:17.960
David Geffen, in the media space,

00:50:17.960 --> 00:50:21.130
probably the most successful
entrepreneur of our time.

00:50:21.130 --> 00:50:25.070
He literally made billions and billions
and billions of dollars from being

00:50:25.070 --> 00:50:30.650
brilliant in the music business, brilliant
in Broadway, successful movie producer.

00:50:30.650 --> 00:50:32.760
It's just, the guy has taste.

00:50:32.760 --> 00:50:35.890
And everything he did, worked.

00:50:35.890 --> 00:50:39.170
And so, but most of all,
a fantastic entrepreneur.

00:50:39.170 --> 00:50:41.660
So, and I'm the builder, right.

00:50:41.660 --> 00:50:45.940
So we had a dreamer,
an entrepreneur, and a builder.

00:50:45.940 --> 00:50:50.290
And those three things
were just incredible, and

00:50:50.290 --> 00:50:52.890
supportive of one another and so.

00:50:52.890 --> 00:50:57.150
Finding that, and
being able to respect that in one another,

00:50:57.150 --> 00:51:03.240
I thought it was a bargain, because in
1994, as I said, I had just been fired.

00:51:03.240 --> 00:51:07.650
Steven Spielberg had won the Academy Award
in the spring of 1994 for

00:51:07.650 --> 00:51:13.090
Schindler's List, and in June of 1994,
he put out the first Jurassic Park.

00:51:13.090 --> 00:51:16.050
So in our world,
he had won both the Academy award and

00:51:16.050 --> 00:51:17.292
the Bank of America award.

00:51:17.292 --> 00:51:18.822
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Something,

00:51:18.822 --> 00:51:21.875
rarely somebody does those
two at the same time.

00:51:21.875 --> 00:51:26.655
David Geffen had actually just
recently sold his music company for

00:51:26.655 --> 00:51:31.872
the third or fourth time, for
another billion dollars, to Universal.

00:51:31.872 --> 00:51:37.301
And when I got fired, I went back to
my office, I left Eisner's office,

00:51:37.301 --> 00:51:41.751
and when I walked back to my,
I did not know this at the time,

00:51:41.751 --> 00:51:45.320
just to give you an idea
how kind of nasty it got.

00:51:45.320 --> 00:51:49.260
So I'm in his office, he goes well I don't
think this is going to work any longer.

00:51:49.260 --> 00:51:52.250
Stating what at that point
was truly the obvious.

00:51:52.250 --> 00:51:54.460
What I didn't know is
while I was in his office,

00:51:54.460 --> 00:51:57.440
he put a press release out saying
that he had fired me, right?

00:51:57.440 --> 00:52:02.240
So I get back to my office, and
literally I walk into my office,

00:52:02.240 --> 00:52:06.710
and my assistant says to me,
Steven Spielberg and

00:52:06.710 --> 00:52:09.780
Bob Zemeckis are on the phone,
and they would not hang up,

00:52:09.780 --> 00:52:14.400
they've insisted on holding on until you
come back there in Jamaica together.

00:52:14.400 --> 00:52:17.900
So, the three of us had made a movie
called Roger Rabbit together, and

00:52:17.900 --> 00:52:23.290
actually had kind of a great bond and, you
know, relationship came out of that movie.

00:52:23.290 --> 00:52:25.100
And so, I get on the phone.

00:52:25.100 --> 00:52:28.090
And they're laughing,
they go my God, this is fantastic.

00:52:28.090 --> 00:52:31.775
It's just great and
it's funny and I'm going, really.

00:52:31.775 --> 00:52:36.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Explain to

00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:41.600
me what I'm missing here because
I'm not feeling any of this, guys.

00:52:41.600 --> 00:52:44.080
And Bob Zemeckis,
I will give him the credit for it.

00:52:44.080 --> 00:52:48.700
Bob Zemeckis says, you know what Geoffrey,
you should just start your own studio.

00:52:49.960 --> 00:52:54.048
And I hung up the phone and I went,
hm, now that's a good idea.

00:52:54.048 --> 00:52:57.365
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Literally, that was the moment.

00:52:57.365 --> 00:53:01.000
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So we're almost out of time.

00:53:01.000 --> 00:53:05.901
To close, I would ask you to
dig the reminiscence gene just

00:53:05.901 --> 00:53:08.410
one more time for us on stage.

00:53:08.410 --> 00:53:14.508
On the day of the September 11th attacks
the entire country is paralyzed,

00:53:14.508 --> 00:53:18.980
in shock, and mourning, and
you spring into action.

00:53:18.980 --> 00:53:21.350
You activate that sense of
personal responsibility and

00:53:21.350 --> 00:53:23.210
you put yourself at the center.

00:53:23.210 --> 00:53:26.319
Can you describe that moment for
us and your leadership-

00:53:26.319 --> 00:53:30.183
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it It was a moment for

00:53:30.183 --> 00:53:34.215
everybody in this country,

00:53:34.215 --> 00:53:39.087
if not around the globe, in which I

00:53:39.087 --> 00:53:44.463
think every single person was not only

00:53:44.463 --> 00:53:50.190
feeling fear, but also helplessness.

00:53:50.190 --> 00:53:52.880
And most of all, what can I do?

00:53:54.360 --> 00:53:59.556
And I thought about that,
I felt like I have to do something,

00:53:59.556 --> 00:54:03.990
I'm not a fireman, I'm not a policeman,

00:54:03.990 --> 00:54:08.100
I'm not soldier,
I'm an entertainer most of all,

00:54:08.100 --> 00:54:13.150
above all what I've tried to do my whole
life Is actually bring laughter to people.

00:54:13.150 --> 00:54:15.310
That's been my mission.

00:54:15.310 --> 00:54:19.900
And if you think about the movies and
the most joyful, rewarding thing for

00:54:19.900 --> 00:54:24.320
me is to stand in the back of a theater
and hear an audience in laughter.

00:54:24.320 --> 00:54:27.632
That's my joy and it's literally the most
beautiful thing in the world to me is to

00:54:27.632 --> 00:54:30.085
hear laughter and
particularly the laughter of children.

00:54:30.085 --> 00:54:38.810
And I thought okay, it would be great if
we could have a shared healing moment.

00:54:40.000 --> 00:54:46.800
And we, my community of entertainers,
actually could do something.

00:54:46.800 --> 00:54:52.592
And so, I had this idea of
doing a telethon that would

00:54:52.592 --> 00:54:58.791
be a tribute to America and
the strength of our country.

00:54:58.791 --> 00:55:03.772
And was able to do the thing I was able
to do, which is to get everybody and

00:55:03.772 --> 00:55:08.589
anybody on the telephone and
talk them into doing something that they

00:55:08.589 --> 00:55:13.140
never would ever think of doing or
would not likely do.

00:55:13.140 --> 00:55:18.530
And so for the first time actually in
history, every single television station,

00:55:18.530 --> 00:55:25.500
every cable station, every radio,
everything, for that two hours, stopped.

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:31.810
While the greatest and
the best entertainers, singers,

00:55:31.810 --> 00:55:37.380
actors, spoke from the heart.

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:42.080
And I just, for me, when I think of all of
the wonderful things that I have been able

00:55:42.080 --> 00:55:45.780
to do and had in my career, honestly,

00:55:45.780 --> 00:55:50.620
nothing even comes remotely close
to that moment and that time.

00:55:50.620 --> 00:55:54.700
In which I felt it was
the ultimate unifying,

00:55:54.700 --> 00:55:58.980
shared moment and just for a second,

00:55:58.980 --> 00:56:03.860
I felt maybe there was, at this tragic,
tragic time, happiness.

00:56:06.030 --> 00:56:09.780
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for the gifts that
you've given us over an incredible career.

00:56:09.780 --> 00:56:10.550
And today,

00:56:10.550 --> 00:56:13.650
in this auditorium, we are so blessed
to hear your lessons on leadership.

00:56:13.650 --> 00:56:17.365
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:56:17.365 --> 00:56:18.360
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

